<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard I of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1157">1157</a>–1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Richard I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, known as Richard the Lionheart, was King of England from 1189 to 1199 and a leading commander of the Third Crusade. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, taking part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and winning victories on the coast, but did not retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> discuss his campaigns as the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> challenge to Saladin, which ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> in 1192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england.jpg" alt="Richard I of England" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Bishop · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_Fontevraud_Abbey_Richard_I_effigy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
